This extension adds AC Odyssey modding support to Vortex Mod Manager.
[summary]Note that AnvilToolkit is required for some mods and will be automatically installed by this extension.[/summary]

This extension will automatically install [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/site/mods/455]AnvilToolkit[/url] and [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/assassinscreedodyssey/mods/42]Forger Patch Manager[/url]. These tools can be run from the Dashboard tab in Vortex.
The extension will also send you a notification about [url=https://www.undertow.club/downloads/resorep.1254]Resorep[/url]﻿, which is a legacy utility for texture injection. The app is behind a login "wall", so it must be downloaded and installed manually if you want to use it.

[b]✅ Supported Versions:[/b]
[list]
[*]Steam[/*]
[*]Epic[/*]
[*]Ubisoft Connect[/*]
[*]Other versions may need to select the installation location manually.[/*]
[/list]

[size=3][b][color=#ea9999]This game does not support symlinks. You must have the mod staging folder and the game on the same drive to use this extension.[/color][/b][/size]

[b]🛠️ Mod Installation Notes:[/b]
[list]
[*]AnvilToolkit installs to the game folder, recognised by an "anviltoolkit.exe" file.[/*]
[*]Forger Patch Manager installs to the game folder, recognised by a "forger.exe" file.[/*]
[*]ResoRep installs to the game folder, recognised by a "d3d11.dll" file.[/*]
[*]Forger Patch mods with a ".forger2" file install to the "ForgerPatches" folder.[/*]
[*]ResoRep Textures mods with a ".dds" file install to the "ResoRep\modded" folder.[/*]
[*]DLC Folder mods with a "dlc_14" folder install to the game folder.[/*]
[*]Extracted Folder mods with an "Extracted" folder install to the game folder.[/*]
[*].forge Folder mods with a "<name>.forge" folder install to the game folder.[/*]
[*].data Folder mods with a "<name>.data" folder install to the game folder.[/*]
[*]Loose Data Files mods with a ".data" file install to the game folder.[/*]
[*]Forge Replacement mods with a ".forge" file install to the game folder.[/*]
[*]Binaries / Root Folder mods with a "videos" folder install to the game folder.[/*]
[*]Any other mod not described above installs to the game folder.[/*]
[*]Archives with a FOMOD installer (a "fomod" folder with ModuleConfig.xml) are handed to Vortex's built-in FOMOD installer instead.[/*]
[/list]
[b]📋 Usage Notes:[/b]
[list]
[*]You can open several useful files/folders/URLs using the buttons within the folder icon on the Mods toolbar.[/*]
[/list]

﻿A few things to note:
[list]
[*][b][size=3]You must have Steam and/or Ubisoft Connect apps open when launching the game from Vortex for the game to start.[/size][/b][/*]
[*][b][size=3]For mods that require AnvilToolkit (ATK), you still need to run the tool from the Dashboard tab in Vortex and unpack/repack the .forge files. Automatic packing of .forge files isn't feasible since it is not possible to predict which .forge files will be used.[/size][/b][/*]
[/list]

[b]📝 Notes for Mod Authors:[/b]
[spoiler]
Packaging rules for Assassin's Creed Odyssey mods, so Vortex installs them to the right place.

Vortex decides what a mod is by looking at the files and folders inside the archive. It tries each installer in order and the first one that matches wins, so archive layout is what determines where your mod ends up.

========================================================================

AnvilToolkit (tool):

This installer handles AnvilToolkit, not mods for it. It exists so users can install AnvilToolkit through Vortex, and mod authors normally never package this.

  Requirements:
    - Recognised by a file named "anviltoolkit.exe" in the archive.

  Installs to: the game folder

  Common mistakes:
    - If you bundle AnvilToolkit inside your mod archive, Vortex treats the whole download as AnvilToolkit rather than as your mod. Ship the mod alone and list AnvilToolkit as a requirement.

========================================================================

Forger Patch Manager (tool):

This installer handles Forger Patch Manager, not mods for it. It exists so users can install Forger Patch Manager through Vortex, and mod authors normally never package this.

  Requirements:
    - Recognised by a file named "forger.exe" in the archive.

  Installs to: the game folder

  Common mistakes:
    - If you bundle Forger Patch Manager inside your mod archive, Vortex treats the whole download as Forger Patch Manager rather than as your mod. Ship the mod alone and list Forger Patch Manager as a requirement.

========================================================================

ResoRep (tool):

This installer handles ResoRep, not mods for it. It exists so users can install ResoRep through Vortex, and mod authors normally never package this.

  Requirements:
    - Recognised by a file named "d3d11.dll" in the archive.
    - Vortex downloads the matching ResoRep package and writes its settings file, so this installer normally only ever runs on that automatic download.

  Installs to: the game folder

  Common mistakes:
    - If you bundle ResoRep inside your mod archive, Vortex treats the whole download as ResoRep rather than as your mod. Ship the mod alone and list ResoRep as a requirement.

========================================================================

Forger Patches:

Patch files for Forger Patch Manager to apply.

  Requirements:
    - Recognised by any file with the ".forger2" extension.

  Installs to: "ForgerPatches"

  Common mistakes:
    - These are applied through Forger Patch Manager, not deployed into the game directly - installing in Vortex alone does not change the game.

========================================================================

ResoRep Textures:

Replacement textures injected at runtime by ResoRep, rather than packed back into a ".forge" archive.

  Requirements:
    - Recognised by any file with the ".dds" extension.

  Installs to: "ResoRep\modded"

  Common mistakes:
    - ResoRep must be installed and its DLL in place for these to show up in game; the textures alone do nothing.
    - Each texture has to keep the name ResoRep dumped it under, or it replaces nothing.

========================================================================

DLC Folder Mods:

Mods laid out as one or more of the game's DLC folders, copied into the game folder with that layout intact.

  Requirements:
    - Recognised by a folder named "dlc_14", "dlc_15", "dlc_16", "dlc_18", "dlc_19", "dlc_20", "dlc_21", "dlc_22", "dlc_23", "dlc_24", "dlc_25", "dlc_26", "dlc_27", "dlc_28", "dlc_29", "dlc_30", "dlc_31", "dlc_35", "dlc_36", "dlc_37", "dlc_38", "dlc_39", "dlc_40", "dlc_42", "dlc_43", "dlc_44", "dlc_45", "dlc_47", "dlc_50", "dlc_52", "dlc_53", "dlc_54", "dlc_55", "dlc_56", "dlc_57", "dlc_58", "dlc_59" or "dlc_60" in the archive.

  Installs to: the game folder

  Common mistakes:
    - Zip the DLC folders themselves, not the folder that contains them - an extra level misplaces every file.

========================================================================

Extracted Forge Content:

Unpacked forge content for AnvilToolkit to repack. The mod is not live until the user runs AnvilToolkit and repacks - deploying it in Vortex only stages the files where the toolkit expects them.

  Requirements:
    - Recognised by a folder named "Extracted" in the archive.

  Installs to: the game folder

  Common mistakes:
    - Content unpacked with an AnvilToolkit older than 1.2.8 cannot be repacked by 1.2.8 or newer, which affects the pre-Unity titles. Repack on the old version first, delete the extracted folder, then unpack again on the new one.

========================================================================

Unpacked .forge Folder:

A whole unpacked ".forge" archive, packaged as a folder named after the archive it came from. It is staged under the extracted-content folder for AnvilToolkit to repack.

  Requirements:
    - Recognised by a folder named "<name>.forge" in the archive.

  Installs to: the game folder

  Common mistakes:
    - Keep the folder named exactly after the ".forge" archive the content came from, extension included. The name is what tells AnvilToolkit which archive to repack into.

========================================================================

Unpacked .data Folder:

An unpacked ".data" file, packaged as a folder named after it. Nothing in the archive says which ".forge" it belongs in, so Vortex stages it under a placeholder folder and asks the user to rename that folder to the right ".forge" name.

  Requirements:
    - Recognised by a folder named "<name>.data" in the archive.

  Installs to: the game folder

  Common mistakes:
    - Name the ".forge" archive your content belongs in somewhere the user will see it - the mod page, a readme, or the archive name. They have to type it into the rename prompt, and Vortex cannot work it out from the files.

========================================================================

Loose Data Files:

Individual data files, staged under a placeholder folder for the user to rename to the ".forge" archive they belong in, the same way an unpacked ".data" folder is.

  Requirements:
    - Recognised by any file with the ".data" extension.

  Installs to: the game folder

  Common mistakes:
    - State which ".forge" archive the files belong in. The user is prompted for that name and has nothing else to go on.

========================================================================

Forge File Mods:

Replacement ".forge" archives, deployed into the game's data folder.

  Requirements:
    - Recognised by any file with the ".forge" extension.

  Installs to: the game folder

  Common mistakes:
    - Forge files must keep their original names to replace the right archive.
    - A forge file belonging to a DLC is routed to that DLC's folder by the "_NN_dlc" segment in its name, so renaming that part of the file name sends it somewhere else. One archive may carry forge files for several DLCs; each is routed on its own.

========================================================================

Root / Game Folder Mods:

For mods laid out the same way the files appear inside the game folder. Vortex copies the matched folder and everything under it straight into the game.

[code]
MyRootMod.zip
└── videos\
    └── ... files in their real relative locations
[/code]

  Requirements:
    - Recognised by a folder named "videos" in the archive.

  Installs to: the game folder

  Common mistakes:
    - Zipping the folder that CONTAINS the game folders, instead of the game folders themselves, adds an extra level and misplaces every file.

========================================================================

Fallback Installer:

The catch-all. Any archive that matched none of the installers above lands here and is copied across unchanged.

[b][color=#ffff00]NOTE: Landing in the fallback installer is a signal your archive layout needs fixing.[/color][/b]

  Requirements:
    - Reaching this installer usually means the archive was not laid out in a way Vortex recognised.
    - Vortex shows the user a notification when a mod installs through the fallback.

  Installs to: the game folder

  Common mistakes:
    - If your mod lands here unintentionally, re-check the layouts above - users will see a fallback warning and may report the mod as broken.

========================================================================

Rules That Apply To Every Mod Type:

    - Archives that contain a FOMOD installer (a "fomod" folder with "ModuleConfig.xml") are handed to Vortex's built-in FOMOD installer instead, and none of the rules above apply.
    - Folder and file name matching is case-insensitive.
    - Extra wrapper folders around a recognised folder are generally fine; the installer searches at any depth.
[/spoiler]

